      <div class="pk-wrap pk-body">
        <section id="itch" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>The itch</h2>
          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--split">
            <div>
              <p>
                In December 2022 I played my grandmother at ohvalhugondi again, and lost again. I decided to write a
                bot that could beat her. I got as far as the game itself: a Python command-line version with the real
                rules, seven pits a side, seven seeds in each, and relay sowing.
              </p>
              <p>
                The bot would have to wait. I didn't yet know how to make a program look ahead, and my grandmother
                passed away before I managed it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <figure class="pk-vis">
              <canvas data-vis="mancalaCli" role="img" aria-label="A text-mode mancala board in a terminal, with players picking pits and the board updating"></canvas>
              <figcaption>The command-line game loop, redrawn</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="does" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What it does</h2>
          <p>
            You pick up every seed in one of your pits and sow them one by one around the board, into your own store
            but never your opponent's. If the last seed lands in a pit that already had seeds, you pick those up too and
            keep going: relay sowing, the part that makes ohvalhugondi so hard to read ahead. A last seed in your own
            store earns another turn, and one in an empty pit on your side captures the pit opposite.
          </p>
          <p>
            Today it's a web game. You can play a friend on the same screen or any of twenty-three bots, from three
            that try to lose to one that searches ahead, picked from a list grouped by strength, and it keeps an Elo
            rating of your own from the games you play against them. There's undo, a hint, a replay of the finished game, and a move log where the search bots say what
            they saw.
          </p>
          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
            <figure>
              <img
                src="assets/projects/mancala/game-light.webp"
                alt="The web game in its paper theme: a wooden board with two rows of seven pits holding drawn seeds, a store at each end, and a move log below"
                width="1800" height="1337" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
              >
              <figcaption>The paper theme, a few moves in</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <img
                src="assets/projects/mancala/game-dark.webp"
                alt="The same board in the ink theme: the bottom player's pits ringed as playable, the stores holding 9 and 15 seeds"
                width="1800" height="1337" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
              >
              <figcaption>The ink theme, the same position</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="works" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <h3>The 2024 bot</h3>
          <p>
            Twenty months after that game I came back to it, because I had finally learned the missing piece:
            depth-first search, which I picked up playing <em>The Farmer Was Replaced</em>, a game where you program a
            farming drone in Python and have to solve its mazes. I worked out how to use it here myself, without AI
            help, and built the bot in Google Colab.
          </p>
          <p>
            It follows its own chains of free turns as deep as they go and keeps the one that ends with the fullest
            store. Today, in TypeScript, it is nine lines:
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code"><b>chain</b>(state, move, depth, me) &#123;
  const r = playTurn(state, move);
  if (!r.freeTurn || depth + 1 &gt;= maxDepth) return score(r.state, me);
  let best = score(r.state, me);
  for (const next of validMoves(r.state)) &#123;
    best = Math.max(best, <b>chain</b>(r.state, next, depth + 1, me));
  &#125;
  return best;
&#125;</pre>
          <p>
            It never thinks about the opponent's reply, and it is still fourth of the twenty-three bots in the game.
            Two of the three above it are built on it: one that also checks your best chain in answer, and one that
            judges positions with a neural network.
          </p>

          <h3>The web game</h3>
          <p>
            The first web version came together in a single day in August 2025, with AI's help on the interface, and
            was split into tidy modules the same day. In July 2026 the rules moved into one place: the engine writes
            down every seed it sows, and the animation only replays that record, so the screen can never disagree with
            the rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            In October 2026 I rebuilt it in TypeScript, again with AI's help. It got tests for every rule, an
            alpha-beta search that runs in the background so the page never freezes, and a design taken from this
            site: the same paper and ink, the same type, a wooden board with drawn seeds. Testing the new version, I
            caught a rule the game had got wrong since its first day. A capture should take only the pit opposite,
            while the seed that lands stays where it is.
          </p>
          <figure class="pk-figure--narrow">
            <img
              src="assets/projects/mancala/design-guide.webp"
              alt="The game's design guide on one sheet: its paper and ink palettes, the two player colours, the wood and seed colours, the board in both themes, pit states, type, buttons and voice"
              width="1324" height="2400" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>The game's design guide, borrowed from this site's</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3>Bots that learn</h3>
          <p>
            Later that month I taught a bot by example. A small neural network, 561 numbers in plain TypeScript,
            learned to judge a position from the full search's verdicts on 200,000 of them. Paired with the 2024 bot's
            chain search, it plays level with the full search in about a quarter of the time. I then wrote the
            network's judgement back out as a formula a person can read, and trained two more networks to lose: they
            sit below the bot that searches ahead to lose.
          </p>
          <p>
            Rating all of them got fast too: every bot plays every other 240 times, on every core at once, in about
            five minutes.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="found" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What the bots found</h2>
          <p>With twenty-three bots, and a solver, the game could finally be measured. Three results stood out.</p>
          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
            <div class="pk-tile">
              <h3>The first move decides it</h3>
              <p>
                With the standard rules, whoever moves first wins by force. Opening with pit 1 starts a chain of free
                turns that passes half the seeds before the other side ever moves. The strongest bot playing itself
                ends 94–4; perfect play, worked out by the solver below, ends 96–2. The game now lets you choose who
                goes first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="pk-tile">
              <h3>A ladder, not exact numbers</h3>
              <p>
                Every bot played every other 240 times, in matched pairs: each opening once from each seat, so neither
                gets the better start. The results were fitted to Elo ratings, each with a range, since a 100–0 pairing
                says one bot is much stronger but not by how much.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <figure class="mc-ladder">
            <ol>
              <li style="--lo: 97.1%; --elo: 98.1%; --hi: 99.5%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Expert <span>Minimax</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">2068</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 96.2%; --elo: 97.5%; --hi: 98.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Wise <span>Learned DFS</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">2058</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 95.4%; --elo: 96.5%; --hi: 97.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Very hard <span>DFS v2</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">2041</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 93.4%; --elo: 94.5%; --hi: 95.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Hard <span>DFS</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">2007</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 88.6%; --elo: 89.7%; --hi: 91.1%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Sharp <span>Shrewd</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1925</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 84.5%; --elo: 85.7%; --hi: 86.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Lucky <span>Monte Carlo</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1857</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 77.8%; --elo: 78.9%; --hi: 80.2%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Tricky <span>Lookahead</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1742</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 73.6%; --elo: 74.7%; --hi: 76%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Thoughtful <span>Planner</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1670</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 67%; --elo: 68%; --hi: 69.3%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Clever <span>Sometimes planner</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1556</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 63.4%; --elo: 64.2%; --hi: 65.4%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Keen <span>Chainer</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1492</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 61.8%; --elo: 62.8%; --hi: 63.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Crafty <span>Spoiler</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1468</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 53.4%; --elo: 54.4%; --hi: 55.4%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Average <span>Greedy</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1324</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 51.1%; --elo: 52.1%; --hi: 53.1%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Careful <span>Turtle</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1285</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 45.2%; --elo: 46.2%; --hi: 47.2%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Relaxed <span>Sometimes greedy</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1185</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 43.8%; --elo: 44.9%; --hi: 46%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Bold <span>Striker</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1163</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 35.3%; --elo: 35.3%; --hi: 35.3%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Easy <span>Random</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">1000</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 28.7%; --elo: 29.9%; --hi: 30.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Thrifty <span>Hoarder</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">909</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 22.6%; --elo: 23.8%; --hi: 25%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Mild <span>Fickle</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">804</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 18.3%; --elo: 19.5%; --hi: 20.5%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Gentle <span>Sometimes generous</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">731</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 8.6%; --elo: 9.8%; --hi: 11.1%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Very easy <span>Generous</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">567</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 4.1%; --elo: 5.2%; --hi: 6.5%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Hopeless <span>Pushover</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">489</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 3.5%; --elo: 4.7%; --hi: 5.9%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Helpless <span>Decompiled pushover</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">480</span></li>
              <li style="--lo: 2%; --elo: 3.3%; --hi: 4.5%"><span class="mc-ladder__name">Doomed <span>Learned pushover</span></span><span class="mc-ladder__track"><i></i><b></b></span><span class="mc-ladder__elo">456</span></li>
            </ol>
            <figcaption>
              The twenty-three bots by Elo, with 90% ranges; Random is pinned at 1000. At the top, the bot that learned
              from the full search plays level with it: from these openings a tie is the best any bot can do.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The ladder holds a few surprises. The 2024 bot, which never considers its opponent, is nearly as strong as
            a full search. A bot that plays thousands of random games from each move is only middling: random games are
            a poor guide in a game this tactical. One early surprise didn't survive better measuring: a bot that always
            plays its leftmost pit seemed to beat one that plays at random three times in four, but once every opening
            was played from both seats the two came out level, 999 to 1000, and it was retired. And at the bottom sit
            bots that play worse than chance, the worst of them a neural network that learned how games are lost.
          </p>

          <h3>Solved, on smaller boards</h3>
          <p>
            Bots can only say who plays better. To know who should win, I wrote a solver that plays perfectly, checked
            seed for seed against the game's own rules. It solves every board up to six pits a side exactly, and most
            with seven, the standard game among them; on bigger boards it narrows the score, or at least proves who
            wins. On almost every board the first player wins by force. The second player wins only on a narrow band of
            small boards with many seeds, and two boards are draws.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="assets/projects/mancala/solved-boards.webp"
              alt="A ten by ten grid of board sizes, pits a side across and seeds per pit up, each square coloured by the first player's share of the seeds with perfect play: red almost everywhere, a narrow blue band where the second player wins, and the standard seven by seven board outlined at 98%"
              width="1924" height="1768" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>Who wins with perfect play, from 1 to 10 pits a side and 1 to 10 seeds a pit</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="bots" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>The bots</h2>
          <p>
            All twenty-three, grouped as the game's opponent list groups them, strongest first, each with its rating and
            the line the game shows for it.
          </p>

          <h3>Strong, 1600 and up</h3>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><b>Expert</b> · Minimax, 2068. Searches ahead with alpha-beta pruning, planning for your replies too.</li>
            <li><b>Wise</b> · Learned DFS, 2058. DFS v2's search, judged by a small neural network that learned from Minimax.</li>
            <li><b>Very hard</b> · DFS v2, 2041. Chains free turns like DFS, then checks your best chain in answer before it commits.</li>
            <li><b>Hard</b> · DFS, 2007. Chains free turns as far as they go. The original 2024 bot.</li>
            <li><b>Sharp</b> · Shrewd, 1925. Lookahead's method, three moves deep instead of two.</li>
            <li><b>Lucky</b> · Monte Carlo, 1857. Plays thousands of random games from each move and picks the one that won most.</li>
            <li><b>Tricky</b> · Lookahead, 1742. Looks two moves ahead and plays one of its better moves, not always the best.</li>
            <li><b>Thoughtful</b> · Planner, 1670. Looks at your best reply, and weighs everything: free turns set up, captures threatened, pits left open.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Club, 1250 to 1600</h3>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><b>Clever</b> · Sometimes planner, 1556. Plans like Planner most of the time; otherwise plays like Greedy.</li>
            <li><b>Keen</b> · Chainer, 1492. Usually takes a free turn or two before banking what it can; otherwise plays like Greedy.</li>
            <li><b>Crafty</b> · Spoiler, 1468. Often plays the move that leaves your best reply weakest; otherwise plays like Greedy.</li>
            <li><b>Average</b> · Greedy, 1324. Takes whatever banks the most seeds this turn.</li>
            <li><b>Careful</b> · Turtle, 1285. Banks what it can, but won't leave a loaded pit open to capture for it.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Learner, 900 to 1250</h3>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><b>Relaxed</b> · Sometimes greedy, 1185. Plays Greedy's move half the time and any legal pit the rest.</li>
            <li><b>Bold</b> · Striker, 1163. Banks what it can, but would rather line up a capture for next turn.</li>
            <li><b>Easy</b> · Random, 1000. Plays any legal pit.</li>
            <li><b>Thrifty</b> · Hoarder, 909. Keeps its seeds on its own side, banking only a little.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Starter, under 900</h3>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><b>Mild</b> · Fickle, 804. Sometimes keeps its seeds at home like Hoarder; more often gives them away like Generous.</li>
            <li><b>Gentle</b> · Sometimes generous, 731. Plays Generous's move most of the time and any legal pit the rest.</li>
            <li><b>Very easy</b> · Generous, 567. Greedy's opposite: banks as little as it can, turning down free turns and captures.</li>
            <li><b>Hopeless</b> · Pushover, 489. Searches ahead like Minimax, but to lose by as much as it can.</li>
            <li><b>Helpless</b> · Decompiled pushover, 480. Pushover's search to lose, judged by a readable formula taken from the losing network.</li>
            <li><b>Doomed</b> · Learned pushover, 456. Pushover's search to lose, judged by a small neural network that learned how games are lost.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Resting</h3>
          <p>Four more are in the code but out of the list: two shelved, two new and waiting for a rating.</p>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><b>Casual</b> · First. Always plays its first pit with seeds, from its own left. Rated level with Random, 999 to 1000, so it added no step to the ladder.</li>
            <li><b>Master</b> · Grandmaster. Minimax with more time: it searches deeper before it moves. Level with Minimax for five times the thinking: searching deeper adds almost nothing here.</li>
            <li><b>Methodical</b> · Rule list. Follows a short list of if/then rules, taken from Intuition's network. Waiting for a full rating; a first one put it near 1680.</li>
            <li><b>Instinctive</b> · Intuition. Names a pit at a glance: a neural network that learned Minimax's moves, with no search at all. Waiting for a full rating; a first one put it near 1200.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="learned" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What I learned</h2>
          <p class="pk-quote">
            The first time I set out to solve a problem that was genuinely hard for me, and did.
          </p>
          <p>
            It's the project I'm proudest of. The idea for the bot came from a game about programming a farm, the bot
            I wrote in 2024 still holds up against a proper search, and measuring the bots showed me the game better
            than playing it: ohvalhugondi looks like a game of patience and plays like a game of chains, where one
            well-chosen pit decides everything.
          </p>
          <p>
            Four years on, the question I started with has an answer, if not the one I hoped for: with perfect play,
            whoever moves first wins, 96 seeds to 2.
          </p>
          <div class="pk-actions">
